(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-book-questions-chapter-09-treatment-quiz"]={"title":"Chapter 9: Treatment Quiz — Book Questions — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Book Questions","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A randomized controlled trial compares two drugs in common use for the treatment of asthma. Three hundred patients were entered into the trial, and eligibility criteria were broad. No effort was made to blind patients to their treatment group after enrollment. Except for the study drugs, care was decided by each individual physician and patient. The outcome measure was a brief questionnaire assessing asthma-related quality of life. Which of the following best describes this trial?","choices":["Pragmatic trial","Large simple trial","Efficacy trial","Equivalence trial","Noninferiority trial"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Every effort was made to make this trial as true to life as possible by comparing drugs in common use, having broad eligibility criteria, not blinding participants, allowing care to proceed as usual, and relying on a patient- centered outcome rather than a laboratory measurement. Although the trial is for efficacy, it is better described as pragmatic and it is certainly not large. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 9.1"},{"q":"A randomized controlled trial compared angioplasty with fibrinolysis for the treatment of acute myocardial infarction. The authors state that “analysis was by intention to treat.” Which of the following is an advantage of this approach?","choices":["It describes the effects of treatments that patients have actually received.","It is unlikely to underestimate treatment effect.","It is not affected by patients dropping out of the study.","It describes the consequences of offering treatments regardless of whether they are actually taken.","It describes whether treatment can work under ideal circumstances."],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Intention-to-treat analysis, counting outcomes according to the treatment group that patients were randomized to, tests the effects of offering treatment, regardless of whether patients actually take it. It is, therefore, about effectiveness in ordinary circumstances and the measure of effect, like usual care, is affected by dropouts, reducing the observed treatment effect over what it would have been if everyone took the treatment they were assigned to. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 9.2"},{"q":"In a randomized trial, patients with meningitis who were treated with corticosteroids had lower rates of death, hearing loss, and neurologic sequelae. Which of the following is a randomized comparison?","choices":["The subset of patients who, at the time of randomization, were severely affected by the disease","Patients who experienced other treatments versus those who did not","Patients who remained in the trial versus those who dropped out after randomization","Patients who responded to the drug versus those who did not","Patients who took the drug compared with those who did not"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. All characteristics at the time of randomization, such as severity of disease, are randomly allocated. Characteristics arising after randomization, such as retention, response to treatment, and compliance, are not. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 9.3"},{"q":"A patient asks for your advice about whether to begin an exercise program to reduce his risk of sudden death. You look for randomized controlled trials but find only observational studies about this question. Some are cohort studies comparing sudden death rates in exercisers with rates of sudden death in sedentary people; others are case control studies comparing exercise patterns in people who had experienced sudden death and matched controls. Which of the following is not an advantage of observational studies of treatments like these over randomized controlled trials?","choices":["Reported effects are for patients who have actually experienced the intervention.","It may be possible to carry out these studies by using existing data that was collected for other purposes.","The results can be generalized to more ordinary, real-world settings.","Treatment groups would have had a similar prognosis except for treatment itself.","A large sample size is easier to achieve."],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The greatest advantage of randomized trials over observational studies is prevention of confounding. Randomization creates comparison groups that would have the same outcome rates, on average, were it not for intervention effects. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 9.4"},{"q":"In a randomized controlled trial of a program to reduce lower extremity problems in patients with diabetes mellitus, patients were excluded if they were younger than age 40, were diagnosed before becoming 30 years old, took specific medication for hyperglycemia, had other serious illness or disability, or were not compliant with prescribed treatment during a run-in period. Which of the following is an advantage of this approach?","choices":["It makes it possible to do an intention-to-treat analysis.","It avoids selection bias.","It improves the generalizability of the study.","It makes an effectiveness trial possible.","It improves the internal validity of the study."],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. The study had extensive inclusion and exclusion criteria. This would increase the extent to which patients in the trial were similar to each other, making it easier to detect treatment differences if they exist, but at the expense of generalizability, the ability to extrapolate from study results to ordinary patient care. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 9.5"},{"q":"Which of the following is not accomplished by an intention-to-treat analysis?","choices":["A comparison of the effects of offering the experimental treatments","A comparison more relevant to effectiveness questions","A comparison of the effects of actually taking the experimental treatments","A randomized comparison of treatment effects"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Intention-to-treat analyses describe the effects of being offered treatments, not necessarily taking them. To describe the effects of actually receiving the intervention, one would have to treat the data as if they were from a cohort study and use a variety of methods to control for confounding. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 9.6"},{"q":"You are reading a report of a randomized controlled trial and wonder whether stratified randomization, which the trial used, was likely to improve internal validity. For which of the following is stratified randomization particularly helpful?","choices":["The study includes many patients.","One of the baseline variables is strongly related to prognosis.","Assignment to treatment group is not blinded.","Many patients are expected to drop out.","An intention-to-treat analysis is planned."],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Stratified randomization is one approach to control of confounding, especially useful when a characteristic is strongly related to outcome—and also when the study is small enough that one worries that randomization might not create groups with similar prognosis. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 9.7"},{"q":"A randomized controlled trial is analyzed according to the treatment each patient actually received. Which of the following best describes this approach to analysis?","choices":["Superiority","Intention-to-treat","Noninferiority","Open label","Per-protocol"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. In per-protocol analyses, outcomes are attributed to the treatment patients actually receive, not the treatment group they were randomized to, which is an intention-to-treat analysis. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 9.8"},{"q":"In a randomized controlled trial, a beta-blocking drug is found to be more effective than placebo for stage fright. Patients taking the beta-blocker tended to have a lower pulse rate and to feel more lethargic, which are known effects of this drug. For which of the following is blinding possible?","choices":["The patients' physicians","The investigators who assigned patients to treatment groups","The patients in the trial","The investigators who assess outcome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Side effects of the drug, both symptoms and signs, would alert patients and doctors to who is taking the active drug but could not affect random allocation, which was done before the drug was begun. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 9.9"},{"q":"Which of the following best describes “equipoise” as the rationale for a randomized trial of two drugs?","choices":["The drugs are known to be equally effective.","One of the drugs is known to be more toxic.","Neither drug is known to be more effective than the other.","Although one drug is more effective, the other drug is easier to take with fewer side effects."],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. For a randomized controlled trial to be ethical, there should not be conclusive evidence that one of the experimental treatments is better or worse than the other—that is, the scientific community should be in a state of “equipoise” on that issue. There may be opinions as to which is better, but no consensus. There may be some evidence bearing on the comparison, but the evidence should not be conclusive. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 9.10"},{"q":"Antibiotic A is the established treatment for community-acquired pneumonia, but it is expensive and has many side effects. A new drug, antibiotic B, has just been developed for community-acquired pneumonia and is less expensive and has fewer side effects, but its efficacy, relative to drug A, is not well established. Which of the following would be the best kind of trial for evaluating drug B?","choices":["Superiority","Crossover","Cluster","Noninferiority","Equivalence"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Because the new drug has advantages over the old one, but comparative effectiveness is unknown, the appropriate randomized trial comparing the two would be a noninferiority trial to establish whether the new drug is no less efficacious. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 9.11"},{"q":"In a randomized controlled trial of two drugs for coronary artery disease, the primary outcome is a composite of acute myocardial infarction, severe angina pectoris, and cardiac death. Which of the following is the main advantage of this approach?","choices":["There are more outcomes events than there would be for any of the individual outcomes.","All outcomes are equally affected by the interventions.","The trial has more generalizability.","Each of the individual outcomes is important in its own right.","If one outcome is infrequent, others make up for it."],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Making the primary outcome of a trial a composite of clinically important and related outcomes increases the number of outcome events and, therefore, the ability of the trial to detect an effect if it is present. The disadvantage is that the intervention may affect the component outcomes differently, and reliance on the composite outcome alone might mask this effect. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 9.12"},{"q":"In a randomized controlled trial comparing two approaches to managing children with bronchiolitis, baseline characteristics of the 200 children in the trial are somewhat different in the two randomly allocated groups. Which of the following might explain this finding?","choices":["“Bad luck” in randomization","A breakdown in allocation concealment","Both","Neither"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. bad luck in randomization and breakdown in allocation concealment (by bad methods or cheating) would show up as differences in baseline characteristics of patients in a trial. Small differences are expected and the challenge is to decide how large the differences must be for them to raise concern. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 9.13"},{"q":"Which of the following is usually learned from a Phase III drug trial?","choices":["The relationship between dose and efficacy","Rates of uncommon side effects","Efficacy or effectiveness","The dose range that is well tolerated"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The usual drug trials reported in the clinical literature are “Phase III” trials, intended to establish efficacy or effectiveness. Further study, with postmarketing surveillance, is needed to detect uncommon side effects. Responses A and D are about what Phase I and Phase II trials are meant to establish. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 9.14"},{"q":"Which of the following is the main advantage of randomized controlled trials over observational studies of treatment effects?","choices":["Fewer ethical challenges","Prevention of confounding","Resemble usual care","Quicker answer","Less expensive"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. is the main advantage of randomized controlled trials, which is why they are valued despite being ethically complicated, slower, and more expensive. Whether they resemble usual care depends on how the trial is designed. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 9.15"}]};
